[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced TypeScript Integration with Vue 3: Generic Components and Volar"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Vue 3 combined with TypeScript and Volar (Vue Language Server) provides a robust development experience. This tutorial explores advanced type-checking features, specifically focusing on generic components in Single File Components (SFCs)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Ensure your project is utilizing Vue 3.3 or higher, as generic component support received major improvements in this release. Make sure your IDE (like VS Code) is using the Vue Official extension (formerly Volar) and has takeover mode enabled or is using the workspace TypeScript version."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Generic Components in Vue 3"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generic components allow you to create reusable components that can accept different data types while maintaining strict type safety. To make a component generic, add the generic attribute to the script setup tag."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"<script setup lang="ts" generic="T extends Record<string, any>">
defineProps<{
  items: T[];
  selectedItem: T;
}>();

const emit = defineEmits<{
  (e: 'select', item: T): void;
}>();
</script>"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Generic Components"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"When consuming the generic component, Volar automatically infers the generic type T based on the props passed down from the parent component. No explicit type arguments are typically required."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"<template>
  <GenericList 
    :items="users" 
    :selected-item="currentUser" 
    @select="handleSelect" 
  />
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
import { ref } from 'vue';
import GenericList from './GenericList.vue';

interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

const users = ref<User[]>([
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }
]);

const currentUser = ref<User>(users.value[0]);

const handleSelect = (user: User) => {
  console.log('Selected:', user.name);
};
</script>"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices with Volar"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use explicit return types for complex computed properties to help Volar infer types faster."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage generic constraints (like "extends Record<string, any>") to restrict types to objects and prevent unexpected primitives."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your tsconfig.json strict mode enabled for maximum type safety across templates and scripts."}]
